artifact.self_contained_problem:
A Regge spacetime is a four-dimensional piecewise linear (PL) manifold equipped with a piecewise linear Lorentzian metric, specified combinatorially by assigning (squared) edge lengths to the 1-simplices of a simplicial triangulation; on each 4-simplex the metric is taken to be flat, and curvature is concentrated as deficit angles on the 2-simplices ("bones"). The Regge equations of motion are the discrete analogue of the vacuum Einstein equations, obtained by varying the Regge action with respect to the edge lengths.

(a) Formulate and prove a precise sense in which a sequence of Regge spacetimes (with edge lengths satisfying the vacuum Regge equations and with mesh size tending to zero, under suitable non-degeneracy or "fatness" conditions on the simplices) converges to a smooth vacuum solution of the Einstein equations Ric(g) = 0. In particular, identify:
  (i) the topology or function space in which the PL metrics, their associated curvature measures, and the Regge action converge to their smooth counterparts;
  (ii) hypotheses on the triangulation (regularity, shape-regularity, causal structure) that guarantee such convergence;
  (iii) error estimates as a function of mesh parameters.

(b) Formulate the analogous convergence question for spacetimes produced by numerical relativity: given a numerical scheme that produces approximate solutions to the vacuum Einstein equations on a discretized spacetime (e.g., finite-difference, finite-element, or spectral discretizations of an evolution formulation such as BSSN or generalized harmonic gauge), state precisely in what sense the discrete approximations converge to a smooth vacuum spacetime as the discretization is refined, including the function space of convergence, required regularity of the limit, and rates of convergence.

